(ns eve.host-completion
  "glojure's completion of its core to Clojure's, loaded first by eve.host (HOST-CONTRACT §10; measured on glojure
   v0.7.22). What glojure's core lacks, or answers otherwise, and eve needs:

   - deftype and reify: glojure has neither (its only runtime type is a defrecord; its protocols are multimethods on
     class, which a record answers with its own type). A deftype here is a record extended to its protocols: its
     fields are the record's, read in a method body as locals, (.-f x) elsewhere (glj-eve's interpreter reads a
     record's field so: adapters/completion/glojure/go/host-field.patch); a mutable field holds a cell (volatile!)
     that ->T makes, its reads deref it and its set! vreset!s it — so such a type is built with ->T, as on clojerl.
     (T. ...) builds a type without mutable fields. A reify is an instance of a record made once for its form, its
     methods closures in the instance.
   - satisfies? and extends?: extend records each type it extends a protocol to, and they read that list
     (instance?: a record type, nil, a class or interface).
   - unchecked-int: glojure's widens instead of wrapping ((unchecked-int 0x80000000) is 2147483648, and an int64 is
     returned as Go's int); here it wraps to a signed 32-bit value, as the JVM's (the other fixed-width ops wrap
     already: measured).
   - + - * (and inc, dec): checked, throwing on i64 overflow as the JVM's do
     (a RuntimeException here: this runtime has no ArithmeticException type)
     (doc/arithmetic-overflow.md; the operator's 2026-11-07 decision), through an exact promoting
     computation — the Go carry binding (math/bits) is the seat's priced follow-on, named in that doc.
   - deref, swap!, reset!, compare-and-set!, swap-vals!, reset-vals!: glojure's take its own atom and IDeref (Go
     interfaces); for a type, they call its IDeref / IAtom below — what eve's types adapter
     (adapters/types/glojure) translates clojure.lang.IDeref and clojure.lang.IAtom onto.
   - realized?: a type's IPending likewise. add-watch, remove-watch, set-validator!, get-validator, alter-meta!
     and reset-meta!: a type's IRef / IReference below (extend-clj's deftype-atom face, extend_clj/core.glj in
     this completion, builds its watches/validator/meta on them).
   - instance? of one of the JVM interfaces deftype translates (clojure.lang.IDeref and kin): glojure answers
     by Go type identity, which a record never has; for a deftype instance it also answers by the translation —
     true when the type was extended to the interface's protocol.
   - sequence with a transducer, dedupe of a collection and partition-all's transducer: glojure's reach JVM classes
     it lacks (clojure.lang.RT.chunkIteratorSeq, a sized java.util.ArrayList); Clojure's here.
   - defprotocol and extend: glojure's protocol is a multimethod per method on class, its impls wrapped in variadic
     fns that apply them (~3 µs a call); a protocol defined after this has fixed-arity method fns, each caching the
     impl it found for a class, and extend writes the protocol's table (glojure's own protocols keep glojure's).
   - a deftype/reify naming a JVM interface (clojure.lang.ILookup and kin, as plain Clojure code writes them):
     the interface goes onto eve.core's protocols, through eve.deftype-proto.proto-map's table — the one eve's
     types adapter translates eve-deftypes by — or onto this completion's own for the atom interfaces.
   - get, assoc, contains?, count and seq on a type: through eve.core's protocols, once eve's types adapter
     names the dispatch (set-coll-dispatch!); a record's own answers are its fields'.

   Calls compiled after this, in any namespace: each is re-rooted (an :inline one loses its inlining first).")

;; The reduce protocols' namespace, loaded here: deftype's map-mode expansion
;; (below) extends clojure.core.protocols/CollReduce and IKVReduce, and glojure
;; loads that namespace only when something requires it — under the test
;; runner clojure.test does; a program that requires eve.atom without it died
;; at eve.map's first map type with an opaque *runtime.RTEvalError
;; ("EvalASTMaybeHostForm: clojure.core.protocols/CollReduce").
(require 'clojure.core.protocols)

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The fixed-width op glojure widens
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(defn- wrap-int
  "x (a number) as a signed 32-bit value."
  [x]
  (let [v (bit-and (long x) 0xFFFFFFFF)]
    (if (>= v 0x80000000) (- v 0x100000000) v)))

(alter-meta! #'clojure.core/unchecked-int dissoc :inline)
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/unchecked-int (fn [_] wrap-int))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; + - * checked (doc/arithmetic-overflow.md)
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The arithmetic contract (the operator's 2026-11-07 decision): checked
;; + - * throw ArithmeticException on i64 overflow on every host, +' -' *'
;; promote, unchecked-* wrap. This binding does not depend on what the
;; seat's own + does at the i64 edge: when both operands are integers in
;; the i64 range, the exact result is computed through the promoting op
;; and a result outside the range throws; any other operands (a value
;; already past i64, a double, a ratio) keep the seat's own answer. The
;; price is REAL (an exact widening computation per integer op, at this
;; seat's interpreted-op prices): the flag binding — math/bits' carry out
;; of Add64/Sub64, a hi-word compare for Mul64 — is Go-level and belongs
;; with eve's Go package for this seat (impls/glojure), named in the doc
;; as this seat's priced follow-on; this binding holds the contract
;; until then.

(def ^:private i64-min -9223372036854775808)
(def ^:private i64-max 9223372036854775807)

(defn- in-i64 [x] (and (integer? x) (<= i64-min x) (<= x i64-max)))

(defn- overflow! []
  ;; glojure's runtime carries no ArithmeticException type (measured: the
  ;; name, bare or java.lang-qualified, does not resolve; IllegalArgument-
  ;; Exception and RuntimeException do, as Go error constructors). The
  ;; throw is ArithmeticException's JVM parent, named here so the seat's
  ;; divergence is a class name, never the semantics.
  (throw (RuntimeException. "integer overflow")))

(defn- checked2
  "core-fn's answer, throwing when two i64-range integers' exact result
   (promote-fn's) leaves the range."
  [core-fn promote-fn]
  (fn [x y]
    (if (and (in-i64 x) (in-i64 y))
      (let [exact (promote-fn x y)]
        (if (or (< exact i64-min) (> exact i64-max)) (overflow!) (core-fn x y)))
      (core-fn x y))))

;; The helpers below capture the seat's fns as VALUES before the
;; re-root and call only the captures. A helper that calls a re-rooted
;; var by name recurses through its own wrapper: neg did exactly that
;; (its body called clojure.core/-, whose new root's 1-arity is neg
;; itself) and unary minus stack-overflowed the runtime — latent on
;; every tree carrying this section, first bitten by a datascript
;; load that calls unary - (glj-interaction lane, 2026-11-07).
(let [add (checked2 clojure.core/+ clojure.core/+')
      sub (checked2 clojure.core/- clojure.core/-')
      mul (checked2 clojure.core/* clojure.core/*')
      orig- clojure.core/-
      orig-' clojure.core/-'
      orig+' clojure.core/+'
      orig-inc clojure.core/inc
      orig-dec clojure.core/dec
      neg (fn [x] (if (in-i64 x)
                    (let [exact (orig-' x)]
                      (if (or (< exact i64-min) (> exact i64-max)) (overflow!) (orig- x)))
                    (orig- x)))
      inc* (fn [x] (if (in-i64 x)
                     (let [exact (orig+' x 1)]
                       (if (> exact i64-max) (overflow!) (orig-inc x)))
                     (orig-inc x)))
      dec* (fn [x] (if (in-i64 x)
                     (let [exact (orig-' x 1)]
                       (if (< exact i64-min) (overflow!) (orig-dec x)))
                     (orig-dec x)))]
  (alter-meta! #'clojure.core/+ dissoc :inline)
  (alter-var-root #'clojure.core/+
    (fn [_] (fn ([] 0) ([x] x) ([x y] (add x y)) ([x y & more] (reduce add (add x y) more)))))
  (alter-meta! #'clojure.core/- dissoc :inline)
  (alter-var-root #'clojure.core/-
    (fn [_] (fn ([x] (neg x)) ([x y] (sub x y)) ([x y & more] (reduce sub (sub x y) more)))))
  (alter-meta! #'clojure.core/* dissoc :inline)
  (alter-var-root #'clojure.core/*
    (fn [_] (fn ([] 1) ([x] x) ([x y] (mul x y)) ([x y & more] (reduce mul (mul x y) more)))))
  (alter-meta! #'clojure.core/inc dissoc :inline)
  (alter-var-root #'clojure.core/inc (fn [_] inc*))
  (alter-meta! #'clojure.core/dec dissoc :inline)
  (alter-var-root #'clojure.core/dec (fn [_] dec*)))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Protocols: a method fn of fixed arities, caching its impl by class
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(defn- answers? [atype x]
  (if (nil? atype) (nil? x) (and (some? x) (instance? atype x))))

(defn impl-for
  "The fn protocol p's method k is for x: the impl extended to x's own type, else to nil (x nil), else to a type x is
   an instance of (Object last) — cached in cache (a volatile map) under x's class."
  [p k x cache]
  (let [m @p
        c (class x)
        fns (:fns m)
        f (or (get-in fns [c k])
              (when (nil? x) (get-in fns [nil k]))
              (some (fn [t] (when (and (some? t) (not (identical? t Object)) (answers? t x)) (get-in fns [t k])))
                    (:impls m))
              (when (some? x) (get-in fns [Object k])))]
    (when-not f
      (throw (IllegalArgumentException.
              (str "No implementation of method: " (name k) " of protocol: " (:name m) " found for class: "
                   (if (nil? x) "nil" (str c))))))
    (vswap! cache assoc c f)
    f))

(defmacro defprotocol
  "A protocol (glojure's own protocol record, marked :eve/fast): each method a fn of the arities its signature lists,
   finding the impl for its first argument's class once (impl-for) and calling it directly after."
  [pname & opts+sigs]
  (let [sigs (filter seq? opts+sigs)]
    `(do (def ~pname (atom {:on-interface true :eve/fast true :name '~(symbol (str *ns*) (name pname))
                            :sigs '~sigs :impls [] :fns {} :caches []}))
         ~@(for [[mname & specs] sigs
                 :let [cache (gensym "cache")
                       arglists (filter vector? specs)]]
             `(let [~cache (volatile! {})]
                (swap! ~pname update :caches conj ~cache)
                (def ~mname
                  (fn ~@(for [al arglists
                              :let [as (vec (repeatedly (count al) #(gensym "a")))]]
                          `(~as ((or (get (deref ~cache) (class ~(first as)))
                                     (impl-for ~pname ~(keyword (name mname)) ~(first as) ~cache))
                                 ~@as)))))))
         (var ~pname))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/defprotocol (fn [_] @#'defprotocol))

;; extend: a fast protocol's table, glojure's for its own; either way the type is recorded (satisfies?, extends?)
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/extend
  (fn [core]
    (fn [atype & proto+mmaps]
      (doseq [[p mmap] (partition 2 proto+mmaps)]
        (swap! p (fn [m] (-> m (update :impls (fn [is] (if (some #(= atype %) is) is (conj (vec is) atype))))
                             (cond-> (:eve/fast m) (update-in [:fns atype] merge mmap))
                             (dissoc :sat))))
        (if (:eve/fast @p)
          (doseq [c (:caches @p)] (vreset! c {}))
          (core atype p mmap))))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The atom, deref and pending interfaces of a type
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(defprotocol IDeref (-deref [o]))
(defprotocol IBlockingDeref (-deref-within [o ms timeout-val]))
(defprotocol IAtom
  (-swap [a f] [a f x] [a f x y] [a f x y more])
  (-reset [a v])
  (-compare-and-set [a old new]))
(defprotocol IAtom2
  (-swap-vals [a f] [a f x] [a f x y] [a f x y more])
  (-reset-vals [a v]))
(defprotocol IPending (-realized? [o]))
(defprotocol IRef
  (-get-validator [o])
  (-set-validator [o vf])
  (-get-watches [o])
  (-add-watch [o k f])
  (-remove-watch [o k]))
(defprotocol IReference
  (-alter-meta [o f args])
  (-reset-meta [o m]))

(declare satisfies?)

(defn- host-ref?
  "x is one of glojure's own (its atom, delay, var, volatile, promise, future ...): a Go value that is no record."
  [x]
  (not (instance? clojure.lang.IRecord x)))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/deref
  (fn [core]
    (fn ([x] (if (and (host-ref? x) (instance? clojure.lang.IDeref x)) (core x) (-deref x)))
      ([x ms timeout-val]
       (if (host-ref? x) (core x ms timeout-val) (-deref-within x ms timeout-val))))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/swap!
  (fn [core]
    (fn ([a f] (if (host-ref? a) (core a f) (-swap a f)))
      ([a f x] (if (host-ref? a) (core a f x) (-swap a f x)))
      ([a f x y] (if (host-ref? a) (core a f x y) (-swap a f x y)))
      ([a f x y & more] (if (host-ref? a) (apply core a f x y more) (-swap a f x y more))))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/reset!
  (fn [core] (fn [a v] (if (host-ref? a) (core a v) (-reset a v)))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/compare-and-set!
  (fn [core] (fn [a o n] (if (host-ref? a) (core a o n) (-compare-and-set a o n)))))

(defn- vals-by-swap
  "[old new] of a swap through a type's IAtom: the old value is the one the successful swap's f was given."
  [a f args]
  (let [seen (volatile! nil)
        nv (apply -swap a (fn [old & xs] (vreset! seen old) (apply f old xs)) args)]
    [@seen nv]))

(defn- host-swap-vals
  "[old new] of a swap of glojure's own atom, by compare-and-set! (glojure's swap-vals! and reset-vals! fail with a
   nil dereference, v0.7.22)."
  [a f args]
  (loop []
    (let [old (clojure.core/deref a) nv (apply f old args)]
      (if (compare-and-set! a old nv) [old nv] (recur)))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/swap-vals!
  (fn [_]
    (fn [a f & args]
      (cond (host-ref? a) (host-swap-vals a f args)
            (satisfies? IAtom2 a) (apply -swap-vals a f args)
            :else (let [[x y & more] args]
                    (vals-by-swap a f (cond (nil? args) [] (nil? (next args)) [x]
                                            (nil? more) [x y] :else [x y more])))))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/reset-vals!
  (fn [_]
    (fn [a v]
      (cond (host-ref? a) (host-swap-vals a (fn [_] v) nil)
            (satisfies? IAtom2 a) (-reset-vals a v)
            :else (vals-by-swap a (fn [_] v) [])))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/realized?
  (fn [core] (fn [x] (if (host-ref? x) (core x) (-realized? x)))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/add-watch
  (fn [core] (fn [x k f] (if (host-ref? x) (core x k f) (-add-watch x k f)))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/remove-watch
  (fn [core] (fn [x k] (if (host-ref? x) (core x k) (-remove-watch x k)))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/set-validator!
  (fn [core] (fn [x vf] (if (host-ref? x) (core x vf) (-set-validator x vf)))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/get-validator
  (fn [core] (fn [x] (if (host-ref? x) (core x) (-get-validator x)))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/alter-meta!
  (fn [core] (fn [x f & args] (if (host-ref? x) (apply core x f args) (-alter-meta x f args)))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/reset-meta!
  (fn [core] (fn [x m] (if (host-ref? x) (core x m) (-reset-meta x m)))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; satisfies? / extends?
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


(defn satisfies?
  "true when protocol p has been extended to x's type (or one x is an instance of)."
  [p x]
  (let [k (if (nil? x) ::nil (class x))
        cached (get (:sat @p) k ::none)]
    (if (identical? cached ::none)
      (let [r (boolean (some #(answers? % x) (:impls @p)))]
        (swap! p assoc-in [:sat k] r)
        r)
      cached)))

(defn extends?
  "true when protocol p has been extended to atype itself."
  [p atype]
  (boolean (some #(= atype %) (:impls @p))))

(intern 'clojure.core 'satisfies? satisfies?)
(intern 'clojure.core 'extends? extends?)

;; The clojure.core$<fn> names: on the JVM they resolve as the AOT classes of clojure.core's fns and
;; portable code uses them as :tag hints (datascript's patch-tag tags 'number as clojure.core$long).
;; glojure has no such classes; the names are interned here as the fns themselves — a tag is a hint,
;; and this completion strips tags before they can act.
(intern 'clojure.core 'clojure.core$long clojure.core/long)
(intern 'clojure.core 'clojure.core$double clojure.core/double)
(intern 'clojure.core 'clojure.core$boolean clojure.core/boolean)

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; deftype
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(def deftype-types
  "The record types deftype and reify made: a type, not a map — the collection predicates answer for one as the JVM's
   do for a deftype (below)."
  (atom #{}))

(def ^:private raw-instance?
  "glojure's own instance?, captured before the bridge below re-roots the var: deftype-instance?
   is called BY that bridge, so it must not go through it (the cycle overflowed the stack at
   load, measured)."
  @#'clojure.core/instance?)

(defn deftype-instance?
  "x is an instance of a type deftype or reify made."
  [x]
  (and (raw-instance? clojure.lang.IRecord x) (contains? @deftype-types (class x))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Collection access on a type: clojure.core's get, assoc, contains?, count
;; and seq answer through eve.core's protocols for a type extended to them
;; (a record's own answers are its fields'). The dispatch is named by eve's
;; types adapter once eve.core exists (set-coll-dispatch!); before that, and
;; for anything not a type, glojure's own answers stand.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(def ^:private coll-dispatch (volatile! nil))

(defn set-coll-dispatch!
  "m: {:lookup f :assoc f :contains-key? f :count f :seq f
       :protocols {:lookup p :assoc p :contains-key? p :count p :seq p}}."
  [m]
  (vreset! coll-dispatch m))

(defn- real-impl?
  "True when protocol p serves x through an impl extended to x's own type
   or to a supertype of it — not through the protocol's Object arm.
   eve.core's protocols extend Object arms that delegate back to
   clojure.core's own fns (ICounted's arm calls clojure.core/count,
   IAssociative's clojure.core/assoc, ISeqable's clojure.core/seq), so a
   satisfies? that counts the arm makes EVERY type satisfy the protocol,
   and routing through the arm re-enters the very fn that asked, without
   bound (measured: eve.core/count of a persistent-sorted-set Iter —
   which has no -count of its own — recursed count -> -count -> count
   until the runtime stack overflowed; the same shape waited in assoc,
   get and seq for any type lacking that kind's impl). impl-for's own
   resolution, minus its Object fallback, is the test."
  [p x]
  (when p
    (let [m @p
          c (class x)]
      (boolean
        (or (get-in (:fns m) [c])
            (some (fn [t] (and (some? t) (not (identical? t Object)) (answers? t x)))
                  (:impls m)))))))

(defn- type-dispatch
  "The dispatch fn of kind for x, when x is a type the kind's protocol has been extended to."
  [kind x]
  (let [d @coll-dispatch]
    (when (and d (deftype-instance? x) (real-impl? (get (:protocols d) kind) x))
      (get d kind))))

(alter-meta! #'clojure.core/get dissoc :inline)
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/get
  (fn [core]
    (fn ([m k] (if-let [f (type-dispatch :lookup m)] (f m k) (core m k)))
        ([m k nf] (if-let [f (type-dispatch :lookup m)] (f m k nf) (core m k nf))))))

(alter-meta! #'clojure.core/assoc dissoc :inline)
;; The var must also carry :redef: glojure's optimizer lowers a direct
;; (clojure.core/assoc ...) call to a native assoc node (its lower-assoc
;; pass), never consulting the var, unless the var's meta has :redef —
;; the compiler's own exemption for redefined core vars. Without the
;; mark, a compiled assoc on a type record-clones it: the clone's cached
;; fields carry the new value but the instance keeps the receiver's slab
;; block, so a value built by assoc publishes the pre-assoc original
;; wherever the slab is read (measured: a DataScript DB built by
;; db-with's assoc/update chain inside a persistent-domain swap
;; materialized as the empty DB it started from). Calls through the var
;; were always routed correctly; the mark makes compiled calls take the
;; same path. The mark must be set before the calling code is analyzed,
;; which the completion's load position (Eve boot, before any port code)
;; guarantees.
(alter-meta! #'clojure.core/assoc assoc :redef true)
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/assoc
  (fn [core]
    (fn ([m] m)
        ([m k v] (if-let [f (type-dispatch :assoc m)] (f m k v) (core m k v)))
        ([m k v & kvs] (let [ret (if-let [f (type-dispatch :assoc m)] (f m k v) (core m k v))]
                         (if kvs (apply clojure.core/assoc ret kvs) ret))))))

(alter-meta! #'clojure.core/contains? dissoc :inline)
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/contains?
  (fn [core]
    (fn [m k] (if-let [f (type-dispatch :contains-key? m)] (f m k) (core m k)))))

(alter-meta! #'clojure.core/count dissoc :inline)
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/count
  (fn [core]
    (fn [x] (if-let [f (type-dispatch :count x)] (f x) (core x)))))

(alter-meta! #'clojure.core/seq dissoc :inline)
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/seq
  (fn [core]
    (fn [x] (if-let [f (type-dispatch :seq x)] (f x) (core x)))))

;; A record is a map to glojure's core: a type is not. map?, coll?, associative?, counted?, seqable? and record? are
;; false for one (eve's shared code tells a host collection from a type by them: d/eve->clj pours a map? into a map,
;; the codec writes a map? as one).
(doseq [v [#'clojure.core/map? #'clojure.core/coll? #'clojure.core/associative? #'clojure.core/counted?
           #'clojure.core/seqable? #'clojure.core/record?]]
  (alter-var-root v (fn [core] (fn [x] (if (deftype-instance? x) false (core x))))))

;; = and hash: glojure's compare a record by its fields and hash them; a type's own equivalence and hash answer for
;; it (as a JVM deftype's equals/hashCode do), once eve's types adapter names them (set-type-equality!: eve.core's
;; -equiv and -hash, for a type extending eve.core's IEquiv / IHash itself). glojure's = is inlined: it loses its
;; inlining first. (Go code in glojure's core compares by its own Equiv still: a type as a host map's key is a record.)

(def ^:private type-equality (volatile! nil))
(def ^:private own-cache (volatile! {}))

(defn set-type-equality!
  "m: {:equiv (fn [x y]) :hash (fn [x]) :equiv-proto p :hash-proto p} — what = and hash call for a type extending
   the protocol itself."
  [m]
  (vreset! own-cache {})
  (vreset! type-equality m))

(defn- own? [k x]
  (when-let [te @type-equality]
    (let [c (class x) ck [k c] hit (get @own-cache ck ::none)]
      (if (identical? hit ::none)
        (let [r (extends? (get te k) c)] (vswap! own-cache assoc ck r) r)
        hit))))

(alter-meta! #'clojure.core/= dissoc :inline :inline-arities)
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/=
  (fn [core]
    (letfn [(eq [x y]
              (cond (and (deftype-instance? x) (own? :equiv-proto x)) (boolean ((:equiv @type-equality) x y))
                    (and (deftype-instance? y) (own? :equiv-proto y)) (boolean ((:equiv @type-equality) y x))
                    :else (core x y)))]
      (fn ([_] true)
        ([x y] (eq x y))
        ([x y & more] (if (eq x y)
                        (if (next more) (recur y (first more) (next more)) (eq y (first more)))
                        false))))))
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/not= (fn [_] (fn ([_] false) ([x y] (not (= x y))) ([x y & more] (not (apply = x y more))))))
;; (a type's own hash is a signed 32-bit value, the JVM's; glojure's hash answers the same bits unsigned)
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/hash
  (fn [core] (fn [x] (if (and (deftype-instance? x) (own? :hash-proto x))
                       (bit-and (long ((:hash @type-equality) x)) 0xFFFFFFFF)
                       (core x)))))

;; glojure's hash-ordered-coll, hash-unordered-coll and mix-collection-hash refer to a Murmur3 class it lacks
;; (v0.7.22): Clojure's algorithm, as glojure's own maps, sets and vectors hash, in eve's Go package (impls/glojure/host).
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/hash-ordered-coll (fn [_] (fn [coll] (eveglj:host.HashOrdered coll))))
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/hash-unordered-coll (fn [_] (fn [coll] (eveglj:host.HashUnordered coll))))
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/mix-collection-hash (fn [_] (fn [hash-basis count] (eveglj:host.MixCollHash hash-basis count))))

;; glojure's range of integers with a negative step walks one element ((seq (range 5 0 -1)) is (5), and reducing it
;; gives nothing, v0.7.22): a lazy seq here (a double range is glojure's, which counts down right)
(defn- down-range [start end step]
  (lazy-seq (when (> start end) (cons start (down-range (+ start step) end step)))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/range
  (fn [core]
    (fn ([] (core)) ([end] (core end)) ([start end] (core start end))
      ([start end step]
       (if (and (neg? step) (integer? start) (integer? step)) (down-range start end step) (core start end step))))))

;; glojure's sequence with a transducer calls clojure.lang.RT.chunkIteratorSeq, which it lacks (so does dedupe of a
;; collection, which is (sequence (dedupe) coll)), and partition-all's transducer builds its buffer as a sized
;; java.util.ArrayList it cannot convert ("int64 to Object[]", v0.7.22, measured 2026-10-20 ET: eve.thread.fold's
;; suites, 47 errors). sequence here is Clojure's lazy one: each input stepped through the transducer only when the
;; items so far are consumed, the completion run once at the end (or when a step answers reduced); partition-all's
;; transducer buffers in a vector.
(defn- xf-sequence [xform inputs step-in]
  (let [out (volatile! [])
        rf (xform (fn ([acc] acc) ([acc x] (vswap! out conj x) acc)))
        drain (fn [] (let [v @out] (vreset! out []) v))
        step (fn step [s]
               (lazy-seq
                (loop [s s]
                  (if (seq @out)
                    (concat (drain) (step s))
                    (if-let [s (seq s)]
                      (if (reduced? (step-in rf (first s)))
                        (do (rf nil) (seq (drain)))
                        (recur (rest s)))
                      (do (rf nil) (seq (drain))))))))]
    (step inputs)))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/sequence
  (fn [core]
    (fn ([coll] (core coll))
      ([xform coll] (xf-sequence xform coll (fn [rf x] (rf nil x))))
      ([xform coll & colls]
       (xf-sequence xform (apply map vector coll colls) (fn [rf xs] (apply rf nil xs)))))))

;; glojure's dedupe reaches its own sequence, not the var's
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/dedupe
  (fn [core] (fn ([] (core)) ([coll] (sequence (core) coll)))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/partition-all
  (fn [core]
    (fn ([n]
         (fn [rf]
           (let [buf (volatile! [])]
             (fn ([] (rf))
               ([result]
                (let [v @buf
                      result (if (seq v) (do (vreset! buf []) (unreduced (rf result v))) result)]
                  (rf result)))
               ([result input]
                (let [v (vswap! buf conj input)]
                  (if (= n (count v)) (do (vreset! buf []) (rf result v)) result)))))))
      ([n coll] (core n coll))
      ([n step coll] (core n step coll)))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Sorted collections: into keeps their order, subseq and rsubseq answer
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

;; glojure's into goes through a transient, which a sorted set's is not ((into (sorted-set) [3 1 2]) is an unordered
;; hash set, v0.7.22): into a sorted collection conjes. Its subseq and rsubseq call seqFrom, which its sorted map and
;; set lack (a nil dereference): they walk seq and rseq, with the collection's comparator (.comparator, nil for
;; compare's; a boolean one as comparator makes it).
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/into
  (fn [core]
    (fn ([] []) ([to] to)
      ;; a deftype source is handed over as its seq: glojure's native into
      ;; coerces a deftype instance as a map of its fields (see the
      ;; seq-coercion routing below)
      ([to from] (if (sorted? to) (reduce conj to from)
                     (core to (if (deftype-instance? from) (seq from) from))))
      ([to xform from] (if (sorted? to) (transduce xform conj to from)
                           (core to xform (if (deftype-instance? from) (seq from) from)))))))

(defn- cmp-of [sc]
  (let [f (.comparator sc)]
    (if (nil? f)
      compare
      (fn [a b] (let [r (f a b)] (if (boolean? r) (cond r -1 (f b a) 1 :else 0) r))))))

(defn- sorted-key [sc e] (if (map? sc) (key e) e))

(defn- bound [sc test k]
  (let [cmp (cmp-of sc)] (fn [e] (test (cmp (sorted-key sc e) k) 0))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/subseq
  (fn [_]
    (fn ([sc test k]
         (let [in? (bound sc test k)]
           (seq (if (#{> >=} test) (drop-while (complement in?) (seq sc)) (take-while in? (seq sc))))))
      ([sc start-test start-key end-test end-key]
       (seq (take-while (bound sc end-test end-key)
                        (drop-while (complement (bound sc start-test start-key)) (seq sc))))))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/rsubseq
  (fn [_]
    (fn ([sc test k]
         (let [in? (bound sc test k)]
           (seq (if (#{< <=} test) (drop-while (complement in?) (rseq sc)) (take-while in? (rseq sc))))))
      ([sc start-test start-key end-test end-key]
       (seq (take-while (bound sc start-test start-key)
                        (drop-while (complement (bound sc end-test end-key)) (rseq sc))))))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The transient family, for a deftype: glojure's transient and its kin
;; Go-assert the value to glojure's own transient interfaces, which a record
;; never implements ("*lang.Record is not ITransientCollection: missing
;; method AsTransient", measured on datascript's (update report :db-after
;; transient)) — an Eve type's transience is eve.core's IEditableCollection
;; / ITransient* protocols, which its impls were translated onto. For a
;; deftype instance satisfying the eve.core protocol, the core fn routes
;; there; every other value keeps glojure's answer. eve.core is resolved
;; lazily: it loads with the first eve-typed deftype, before portable code
;; transients one.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(defn- eve-proto
  "eve.core's protocol of this name, or nil when eve.core is not loadable here."
  [sym]
  (try (require 'eve.core)
       (when-let [v (resolve (symbol "eve.core" (name sym)))] @v)
       (catch Throwable _ nil)))

(defn- eve-method
  "eve.core's protocol fn of this name, or nil."
  [sym]
  (try (require 'eve.core)
       (resolve (symbol "eve.core" (name sym)))
       (catch Throwable _ nil)))

(defn- route-transient
  "core wrapped so a deftype instance satisfying eve.core's protocol proto is answered by its
   method m; any other value by core."
  [core proto m]
  (fn [& xs]
    (let [x (first xs)]
      (if (and (deftype-instance? x)
               (when-let [p (eve-proto proto)] (satisfies? p x))
               (eve-method m))
        (apply @(eve-method m) xs)
        (apply core xs)))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/transient   (fn [core] (route-transient core 'IEditableCollection '-as-transient)))
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/persistent! (fn [core] (route-transient core 'ITransientCollection '-persistent!)))
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/conj!       (fn [core] (route-transient core 'ITransientCollection '-conj!)))
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/assoc!      (fn [core] (route-transient core 'ITransientAssociative '-assoc!)))
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/dissoc!     (fn [core] (route-transient core 'ITransientMap '-dissoc!)))
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/disj!       (fn [core] (route-transient core 'ITransientSet '-disjoin!)))
(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/pop!        (fn [core] (route-transient core 'ITransientVector '-pop!)))

;; Seq coercion routing: glojure's native first/rest/next/take coerce their
;; argument through the host's seq machinery, which treats a completion
;; deftype instance as a map of its fields (a persistent-sorted-set Iter
;; yields [:set ..] [:left ..] [:right ..]) instead of calling its
;; ISeqable -seq. clojure.core/seq itself routes correctly on this seat,
;; so for deftype instances these four answer through (seq x) via the
;; captured core fns; every other value keeps the host answer.
(let [core-first @#'clojure.core/first
      core-rest  @#'clojure.core/rest
      core-next  @#'clojure.core/next
      core-take  @#'clojure.core/take]
  (alter-var-root #'clojure.core/first
    (fn [_] (fn [x] (if (deftype-instance? x) (core-first (seq x)) (core-first x)))))
  (alter-var-root #'clojure.core/rest
    (fn [_] (fn [x] (if (deftype-instance? x) (core-rest (seq x)) (core-rest x)))))
  (alter-var-root #'clojure.core/next
    (fn [_] (fn [x] (if (deftype-instance? x) (core-next (seq x)) (core-next x)))))
  (alter-var-root #'clojure.core/take
    (fn [_] (fn [n x] (if (deftype-instance? x) (core-take n (seq x)) (core-take n x))))))

;; partition-by has the same defect inside its own body: it walks its
;; source with the host's first/rest, so a deftype source (a
;; persistent-sorted-set Iter, as datascript's -search returns) is
;; traversed as a map of its fields — every element's key fn reads nil
;; (measured: datascript's entity cache came out {nil nil} and every
;; entity lookup missed, which wedged the flagship worker at its
;; rendezvous). Hand a deftype source over as its seq, as above.
(let [core-partition-by @#'clojure.core/partition-by]
  (alter-var-root #'clojure.core/partition-by
    (fn [_] (fn [f coll] (if (deftype-instance? coll)
                           (core-partition-by f (seq coll))
                           (core-partition-by f coll))))))

;; mapv likewise (measured in the same probe): its body reaches the
;; source through into/reduce as the host built them, coercing a
;; deftype source as a field map. Build it over the seq instead.
(let [core-mapv @#'clojure.core/mapv]
  (alter-var-root #'clojure.core/mapv
    (fn [_] (fn ([f coll] (if (deftype-instance? coll)
                            (core-mapv f (seq coll))
                            (core-mapv f coll)))
                ([f c1 c2] (core-mapv f c1 c2))
                ([f c1 c2 & colls] (apply core-mapv f c1 c2 colls))))))

;; reduce has the same native-coercion defect as first/take: over a
;; completion deftype instance it walks the instance's fields as a map
;; instead of its seq (a persistent-sorted-set yields [:root ..]
;; [:shift ..] [:cnt ..] field entries). A deftype instance is therefore
;; reduced over (seq x) via the captured core reduce — same elements,
;; same order as its IReduceInit would give; other values keep the
;; host answer.
(let [core-reduce @#'clojure.core/reduce]
  (alter-var-root #'clojure.core/reduce
    (fn [_] (fn ([f coll] (if (deftype-instance? coll)
                            (core-reduce f (seq coll))
                            (core-reduce f coll)))
                ([f init coll] (if (deftype-instance? coll)
                                 (core-reduce f init (seq coll))
                                 (core-reduce f init coll)))))))

;; count likewise: over a deftype instance that is not eve.core/ICounted
;; the native count coerces it as a field map (and can recurse without
;; bound on an iterator over a set); a counted deftype keeps the host
;; answer, any other deftype instance counts its seq. "Counted" must be
;; real-impl?'s test, not satisfies?: eve.core extends ICounted to
;; Object with an arm that itself calls clojure.core/count, so every
;; deftype satisfies ICounted, and a satisfies? guard routes an
;; uncounted type (a sorted-set Iter) count -> -count -> the arm ->
;; count without bound.
(let [core-count @#'clojure.core/count]
  (alter-var-root #'clojure.core/count
    (fn [_] (fn [x] (if (and (deftype-instance? x)
                             (not (when-let [p (eve-proto 'ICounted)] (real-impl? p x))))
                      (core-count (seq x))
                      (core-count x))))))

(defn- mutable-field? [f]
  (let [m (meta f)] (or (:mutable m) (:unsynchronized-mutable m) (:volatile-mutable m))))

(defn- binding-syms
  "The symbols a binding form (a symbol, or a destructuring vector or map) binds."
  [b]
  (cond (symbol? b) (if (= '& b) [] [b])
        (vector? b) (mapcat binding-syms b)
        (map? b) (concat (mapcat binding-syms (remove keyword? (keys b)))
                         (mapcat (fn [[k v]] (when (#{:keys :syms :strs} k) (map #(symbol (name %)) v))) b)
                         (when-let [a (:as b)] [a]))
        :else []))

(def ^:private bound-forms
  "The forms whose binding vector the cell walk reads (a name they bind shadows a field)."
  '#{let let* loop loop* when-let if-let when-some if-some when-first binding
     clojure.core/let clojure.core/loop clojure.core/when-let clojure.core/if-let
     clojure.core/when-some clojure.core/if-some clojure.core/binding})

(defn- cells
  "A method body with each cell field (a symbol in cs) read through deref and set! through vreset!: a name a let,
   loop, fn or binding form binds is left alone where it is bound."
  [form cs]
  (letfn [(walk [form shadow]
            (cond
              (and (symbol? form) (contains? cs form) (not (contains? shadow form)))
              (list 'clojure.core/deref form)

              (not (seq? form))
              (cond (vector? form) (with-meta (mapv #(walk % shadow) form) (meta form))
                    (map? form) (with-meta (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [(walk k shadow) (walk v shadow)])) form)
                                  (meta form))
                    (set? form) (with-meta (set (map #(walk % shadow) form)) (meta form))
                    :else form)

              (empty? form) form
              (contains? '#{quote var} (first form)) form

              ;; a macro call is expanded first (a cell field it names, (caching-hash this f hash__), is the place it
              ;; set!s after expansion, not a read)
              (and (symbol? (first form)) (not (contains? shadow (first form))) (not (special-symbol? (first form)))
                   (not (contains? bound-forms (first form)))
                   (when-let [v (resolve (first form))] (and (var? v) (:macro (meta v)))))
              (walk (macroexpand-1 form) shadow)

              (and (= 'set! (first form)) (symbol? (second form)) (contains? cs (second form))
                   (not (contains? shadow (second form))))
              (list 'clojure.core/vreset! (second form) (walk (nth form 2) shadow))

              (contains? bound-forms (first form))
              (let [[op bvec & body] form
                    [bs shadow'] (reduce (fn [[acc sh] [b init]]
                                           [(conj acc b (walk init sh)) (into sh (binding-syms b))])
                                         [[] shadow] (partition 2 bvec))]
                (with-meta (list* op bs (map #(walk % shadow') body)) (meta form)))

              (contains? '#{fn fn* clojure.core/fn} (first form))
              (let [[op & more] form
                    [nm more] (if (symbol? (first more)) [(first more) (rest more)] [nil more])
                    sh (cond-> shadow nm (conj nm))]
                (with-meta (concat (cond-> [op] nm (conj nm))
                                   (if (vector? (first more))
                                     (let [[args & body] more sh (into sh (binding-syms args))]
                                       (cons args (map #(walk % sh) body)))
                                     (map (fn [[args & body]]
                                            (let [sh (into sh (binding-syms args))]
                                              (list* args (map #(walk % sh) body))))
                                          more)))
                  (meta form)))

              :else (with-meta (apply list (map #(walk % shadow) form)) (meta form))))]
    (walk form #{})))

(defn- occurs?
  "Whether symbol s occurs anywhere in form (a field a body never names is not bound for it)."
  [s form]
  (cond (= s form) true
        (or (seq? form) (vector? form) (set? form)) (boolean (some #(occurs? s %) form))
        (map? form) (boolean (some #(or (occurs? s (key %)) (occurs? s (val %))) form))
        :else false))

(defn- untag [x] (if (and (symbol? x) (meta x)) (with-meta x nil) x))

(defn- parse-impls
  "deftype's body, protocol then its methods, as [[protocol [(name [args] body...) ...]] ...]."
  [specs]
  (loop [out [] s specs]
    (if (seq s)
      (let [p (first s) [ms more] (split-with seq? (rest s))]
        (recur (conj out [p (vec ms)]) more))
      out)))

(defn- method-arities
  "A protocol's methods as extend-type takes them: one entry per name, its arities from every entry with that name
   (deftype lists an arity per entry), each body with the fields it names bound from this."
  [methods fields cs]
  (for [[nm entries] (group-by first methods)]
    (cons nm
          (for [[_ args & body] entries
                :let [args (mapv untag args)
                      this (first args)
                      shadow (set (binding-syms args))
                      used (filter #(and (not (contains? shadow %)) (occurs? % body)) fields)
                      body (map #(cells % (apply disj cs shadow)) body)]]
            (list args
                  (if (seq used)
                    (list* 'clojure.core/let (vec (mapcat (fn [f] [f (list (symbol (str ".-" f)) this)]) used))
                           body)
                    (cons 'do body)))))))

(def ^:private dropped-protocols
  "What a deftype here takes no impls for: Object's methods (a record's equality, hash and printing are glojure's;
   eve's types adapter gives a type a printer of its own)."
  '#{Object java.lang.Object})

(def ^:private completion-interface-map
  "The JVM interfaces whose impls go onto this completion's own protocols, each method's name there — the
   same table eve's types adapter keeps for eve-deftypes (adapters/types/glojure: completion-interfaces)."
  '{clojure.lang.IDeref {:protocol eve.host-completion/IDeref :methods {deref -deref}}
    clojure.lang.IBlockingDeref {:protocol eve.host-completion/IBlockingDeref :methods {deref -deref-within}}
    clojure.lang.IAtom {:protocol eve.host-completion/IAtom
                        :methods {swap -swap reset -reset compareAndSet -compare-and-set}}
    clojure.lang.IAtom2 {:protocol eve.host-completion/IAtom2 :methods {swapVals -swap-vals resetVals -reset-vals}}
    clojure.lang.IPending {:protocol eve.host-completion/IPending :methods {isRealized -realized?}}
    clojure.lang.IRef {:protocol eve.host-completion/IRef
                       :methods {getValidator -get-validator setValidator -set-validator
                                 getWatches -get-watches addWatch -add-watch removeWatch -remove-watch}}
    clojure.lang.IReference {:protocol eve.host-completion/IReference
                             :methods {alterMeta -alter-meta resetMeta -reset-meta}}})

(defn- proto-map-lookup
  "eve.deftype-proto.proto-map's lookup, loaded on first use: the canonical CLJ -> CLJS protocol table
   eve's types adapter translates eve-deftypes by. nil when it cannot be loaded."
  [sym]
  (try (require 'eve.deftype-proto.proto-map)
       ((resolve 'eve.deftype-proto.proto-map/lookup) sym)
       (catch Throwable _ nil)))

(defn- rename-methods
  "methods ((name args & body) ...) with each name renamed through renames {old new}; a name with no
   entry is dropped (it has no counterpart on this seat)."
  [methods renames]
  (keep (fn [[nm & more]] (when-let [n (get renames nm)] (cons n more))) methods))

(defn- translate-impl
  "One [protocol methods] impl as this seat takes it: [[protocol methods] ...] and whether an entry names
   an eve.core protocol. A JVM interface goes onto eve.core's protocols, through proto-map's table
   (methods renamed, a split protocol split, a CLJ-only one dropped), or onto this completion's own for
   the atom interfaces; any other protocol passes as written."
  [[p ms]]
  (cond
    (contains? completion-interface-map p)
    (let [entry (get completion-interface-map p)]
      [[[(:protocol entry) (rename-methods ms (:methods entry))]] false])

    :else
    (if-let [mapping (proto-map-lookup p)]
      (cond (:clj-only? mapping) [[] false]
            (:marker? mapping) [[[(symbol "eve.core" (name (:cljs-proto mapping))) []]] true]
            :else
            [(for [[proto ms2] (group-by (fn [[nm]] (when-let [info (get (:methods mapping) nm)]
                                                      (or (:cljs-proto-override info) (:cljs-proto mapping))))
                                         ms)
                   :when proto]
               [(symbol "eve.core" (name proto))
                (keep (fn [[nm & more]] (when-let [cn (:cljs-name (get (:methods mapping) nm))]
                                          (cons cn more))) ms2)])
             true])
      (if (and (symbol? p)
               (nil? (namespace p))
               (.contains (name p) "."))
        ;; a JVM class with no table entry and no completion entry (clojure.lang.IKeywordLookup,
        ;; a lookup-thunk optimization with no CLJS counterpart): a class name is one symbol
        ;; with dots and no namespace, it names no var on this seat and can never resolve —
        ;; dropped, the :clj-only disposition. A protocol a user defined is a plain or
        ;; ns-qualified name without dots and passes as written, below.
        [[] false]
        [[[p ms]] false]))))

(defn- translate-impls
  "A deftype/reify's impls as this seat takes them (translate-impl on each): [impls eve-core?]."
  [impls]
  (reduce (fn [[out core?] impl]
            (let [[entries c?] (translate-impl impl)]
              [(into out entries) (or core? c?)]))
          [[] false] impls))

(defmacro deftype
  "(deftype T [fields...] Protocol (method [this ...] body...) ...): a record extended to its protocols (above). ->T
   and T__make build one, a cell made for each mutable field. With ^:eve/map on T it stands in for a map: a Go value
   glojure's core takes as one (eve's Go types package, impls/glojure/types: its map methods call the glj functions
   eve's types adapter names), its record type field-less (no key reads a field), (T. ...) made by its factory."
  [type-name fields & specs]
  (let [map-mode? (:eve/map (meta type-name))
        type-name (vary-meta type-name dissoc :eve/map)
        names (mapv untag fields)
        cs (set (map untag (filter mutable-field? fields)))
        [impls needs-eve-core?] (translate-impls (remove #(contains? dropped-protocols (first %)) (parse-impls specs)))
        args (mapv #(symbol (str "a" %)) (range (count names)))
        factory `(fn ~args (new ~type-name ~@(map (fn [f a] (if (contains? cs f) `(clojure.core/volatile! ~a) a))
                                                 names args)))]
    `(do ~(if map-mode?
            `(do (clojure.core/defrecord ~type-name [])
                 (eveglj:types.Define ~type-name ~(mapv str names))
                 ;; glojure's reduce protocols have no case for a record type: a map type's are its own
                 (clojure.core/extend-type ~type-name
                   clojure.core.protocols/CollReduce
                   (~'coll-reduce ([c# f#] (.Reduce c# f#)) ([c# f# v#] (.ReduceInit c# f# v#)))
                   clojure.core.protocols/IKVReduce
                   (~'kv-reduce [c# f# init#] (reduce (fn [a# e#] (f# a# (key e#) (val e#))) init# c#))))
            `(do (clojure.core/defrecord ~type-name ~names)
                 (swap! deftype-types conj ~type-name)))
         ~@(when needs-eve-core? [`(clojure.core/require 'eve.core)])
         (def ~(symbol (str (name type-name) "__make")) ~factory)
         ~@(when (or map-mode? (seq cs)) [`(def ~(symbol (str "->" (name type-name))) ~factory)])
         ~@(for [[p methods] impls]
             `(clojure.core/extend-type ~type-name ~p ~@(method-arities methods names cs)))
         ~type-name)))

(intern 'clojure.core (with-meta 'deftype {:macro true}) @#'deftype)
(.SetMacro #'clojure.core/deftype)

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; instance? of a JVM interface, for a type: the identity half of the
;; interface translation above. glojure's instance? answers by Go type
;; identity, which a record never has for clojure.lang.IDeref and kin —
;; so (instance? clojure.lang.IDeref x) is false for every deftype here,
;; however it was extended (measured: a completion deftype implementing
;; IDeref, and datascript.lru's type, both false; glojure's own atom
;; true). Portable code gates on it (datascript.conn's conn?). For the
;; interfaces this completion itself translates, and only for a deftype
;; instance, instance? also answers by the translation: true when the
;; type was extended to the interface's protocol. Everything else keeps
;; glojure's answer. (instance? is var-routed on this seat, measured.)
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(def ^:private iface-classes
  "The JVM interface symbols completion-interface-map translates, as [symbol class] pairs. (resolve
   alone, not deref: two of the map's keys name this file's own protocols — their vars' roots are
   the protocol atoms, and derefing the class the symbol also names would dispatch -deref on it.)"
  (into {} (keep (fn [s] (when-let [v (resolve s)] [s (if (var? v) @v v)])) (keys completion-interface-map))))

(alter-var-root #'clojure.core/instance?
  (fn [core]
    (fn [c x]
      (or (core c x)
          (boolean
           (and (deftype-instance? x)
                (some (fn [[s cls]]
                        (and (identical? c cls)
                             (let [p (resolve (:protocol (get completion-interface-map s)))]
                               (and p (extends? @p (class x))))))
                      iface-classes)))))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; reify
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(def ^:private reify-types (atom {}))

(def ^:private fn-interfaces
  "The interfaces a reify of nothing else is a function: a comparator and the fn interfaces. Such a reify
   is the fn itself on this seat (its compare/invoke bodies the fn's arities)."
  '#{java.util.Comparator clojure.lang.IFn IFn$OOL clojure.lang.IFn$OOL})

(defmacro reify
  "(reify Protocol (method [this ...] body...) ...): an instance of a record made once for this form (at its
   expansion), extended to the protocols with methods that call the instance's closures. A reify of only
   the fn interfaces (fn-interfaces) is a plain fn of its compare (else invoke) arities."
  [& specs]
  (let [raw-impls (remove #(contains? dropped-protocols (first %)) (parse-impls specs))]
    (if (and (seq raw-impls)
             (every? (fn [[p]] (or (contains? fn-interfaces p) (= "IFn$OOL" (name p)))) raw-impls))
      (let [methods (mapcat second raw-impls)
            ;; method names arrive as written for a hand-written reify, but a macro-generated one
            ;; (datascript's defcomp, a syntax-quote template) carries them qualified —
            ;; clojure.core/compare, datascript.db/invoke. Match by name, as for IFn$OOL above:
            ;; matching the full symbol selects no arity and emits (fn) with no parameter
            ;; declaration at all (the analyzer's "Parameter declaration missing" on datascript.db).
            compares (filter #(= "compare" (name (first %))) methods)
            chosen (if (seq compares) compares (filter #(= "invoke" (name (first %))) methods))]
        `(clojure.core/fn ~@(for [[_ args & body] chosen]
                              (list* (mapv untag (rest args)) body))))
      (let [[impls needs-eve-core?] (translate-impls raw-impls)
        k [(str *ns*) (pr-str specs)]
        tname (or (get @reify-types k)
                  (let [t (gensym "reify__")]
                    (eval `(do (clojure.core/defrecord ~t [~'fns])
                               (swap! deftype-types conj ~t)
                               ~@(when needs-eve-core? [`(clojure.core/require 'eve.core)])
                               ~@(for [[p methods] impls]
                                   `(clojure.core/extend-type ~t ~p
                                      ~@(for [[nm entries] (group-by first methods)]
                                          (cons nm (for [[_ args] entries
                                                         :let [as (vec (repeatedly (count args) #(gensym "a")))]]
                                                     (list as (list* (list 'clojure.core/get (list '.-fns (first as))
                                                                           (list 'quote nm))
                                                                     as)))))))))
                    (swap! reify-types assoc k t)
                    t))
        fns (into {} (for [[_ methods] impls [nm entries] (group-by first methods)]
                       [(list 'quote nm)
                        (cons 'clojure.core/fn (for [[_ args & body] entries] (list* (mapv untag args) body)))]))]
    `(new ~(symbol (str *ns*) (name tname)) ~fns)))))

(intern 'clojure.core (with-meta 'reify {:macro true}) @#'reify)
(.SetMacro #'clojure.core/reify)

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; defrecord with protocol implementations: glojure's defrecord is its one
;; runtime type, but it refuses impls at expansion ("defrecord protocol
;; implementations are not yet supported") — the wall datascript.db's
;; defrecord-updatable (FilteredDB) hits, since that macro macroexpands
;; (defrecord name fields impls) and postwalks the expansion for a deftype*
;; form to dedupe against the impls it was given. Here defrecord keeps
;; glojure's record — the base expansion is the original macro's own,
;; applied at expansion time as macroexpand would — and the impls are
;; translated exactly as deftype's are (JVM interfaces onto this
;; completion's / eve.core's protocols) and extended after the record:
;; the port's own cljs shape, (defrecord name fields) + (extend-type
;; name impls...). The expansion is a do of those forms: no deftype*
;; for the dedupe postwalk to find, and the impls are the record's own.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(def ^:private glojure-defrecord
  "clojure.core/defrecord's macro fn, captured before the intern below: the base expansion of a
   record with no impls is this fn's own work (it makes the record class at expansion time)."
  @#'clojure.core/defrecord)

(defmacro defrecord
  "(defrecord T [fields...] Protocol (method [this ...] body...) ...): glojure's record, with any
   protocol implementations translated as deftype's are and extended onto the record."
  [type-name fields & specs]
  (let [names (mapv untag fields)
        [impls needs-eve-core?] (translate-impls (remove #(contains? dropped-protocols (first %)) (parse-impls specs)))
        base (apply glojure-defrecord &form &env [type-name fields])]
    `(do ~base
         ~@(when needs-eve-core? [`(clojure.core/require 'eve.core)])
         ~@(for [[p methods] impls]
             `(clojure.core/extend-type ~type-name ~p ~@(method-arities methods names #{})))
         ~type-name)))

(intern 'clojure.core (with-meta 'defrecord {:macro true}) @#'defrecord)
(.SetMacro #'clojure.core/defrecord)

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; This completion's own root, last on the load path: glojure's loader
;; scans every load-path root for a namespace's file and the LAST root
;; holding it supplies it (its load loop overwrites as it goes; measured
;; on this seat, and read in the runtime's load: roots in order, .glj /
;; .clj / .cljc within each). A consumer's classpath puts its own
;; libraries after eve's roots, so a library file of the same namespace
;; shadows this completion's — extend_clj/core.cljc (DataScript's
;; dependency, whose deftype-atom emits deftype*) over this completion's
;; extend_clj/core.glj (the W5 adapter), clojure.set / data / edn
;; likewise wherever a library carries them. The seat contract is that
;; this completion loads first; from here it re-appends its own root —
;; found among the harness's GLJ_CLASSPATH entries by this tree's
;; layout — so the completion's answers are the ones later loads get.
;; A harness that does not set GLJ_CLASSPATH keeps the old behavior.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(try
  (when-let [cp (os/Getenv "GLJ_CLASSPATH")]
    (require 'clojure.string)
    (doseq [root (clojure.string/split cp #":")]
      (when (or (.endsWith root "/adapters/completion/glojure/src")
                (.endsWith root "/adapters/completion/glojure/src/"))
        (add-load-path root))))
  (catch Throwable _ nil))

;; clojure.lang.RT's statics, answered by vars of that namespace (clojure/lang/RT.glj in this
;; completion): portable code calls (clojure.lang.RT/assoc m k v) in static syntax — datascript's
;; inline assoc expands to exactly that — and the analyzer resolves the call to the var.
(require 'clojure.lang.RT)

;; clojure.lang.Util's statics the same way (clojure/lang/Util.glj in this completion):
;; hasheq and hashCombine, which datascript's ihash and datom hash call.
(require 'clojure.lang.Util)

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; ClassCastException, for catch: glojure's class registry has no
;; ClassCastException (measured: IllegalArgumentException, Exception,
;; RuntimeException, Throwable resolve; ClassCastException does not, in
;; either short or java.lang form), and portable code catches it around
;; compare — datascript's value-compare falls back to a class ordering
;; when compare cannot compare its values. A catch class resolves
;; through the namespace mapping when one exists (measured), so
;; clojure.core gains the name, bound to Throwable's class: on this seat
;; a failed compare throws a runtime error, and the fallback the catch
;; guards is the same. Only the name is supplied; no code catches
;; anything it did not already catch around a compare.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(intern 'clojure.core 'ClassCastException (resolve 'Throwable))

;; Iterable, for instance?: the registry has no java.lang.Iterable either (measured; java.util.Map,
;; List, Set and Collection all resolve), and portable code tests it as a fallback disjunct —
;; datascript's seqable? already answers every value this seat has through Seqable, arrays and
;; java.util.Map before Iterable is reached. The name is bound to java.util.Iterator's class, a
;; host type no seat collection is an instance of, so the test answers false exactly where the
;; JVM's would answer false for a Clojure value.
(intern 'clojure.core 'Iterable (resolve 'java.util.Iterator))

;; Eduction: glojure's clojure.core has no eduction at all (measured: eduction, ->Eduction and
;; clojure.lang.Eduction all unresolvable), where clojure.core itself defines the Eduction
;; deftype and portable code constructs it directly — datascript's -datoms and query tuple
;; collection both do. The completion supplies the two names over sequence: for the pure,
;; finite transformations portable code educes, a sequence of the transducer over the
;; collection answers with the same elements, in the same order, to seq, reduce and into;
;; what differs from the JVM's Eduction is only the realization strategy (a sequence caches
;; what it realizes; an eduction re-runs), never the results.
(intern 'clojure.core '->Eduction
        (fn [xform coll] (sequence xform coll)))

(intern 'clojure.core 'eduction
        (fn ([xform coll] (sequence xform coll))
          ([xform coll & xforms] (sequence (apply comp xform xforms) coll))))
